  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The June 17 digital signing of a 14-point US–Iran memorandum of understanding by presidents Trump and Pezeshkian — following a 60-day ceasefire extension agreed in writing around June 12 — constitutes the most consequential diplomatic development since Iran suspended mediated talks on June 1. The MOU established four working groups on sanctions termination, nuclear affairs, reconstruction, and monitoring. The signing occurred after IDF strikes in Beirut on June 14 prompted a US-mediated standdown of an Iranian retaliation that had been prepared, and one day after the close of the <span class="cite">F1</span> resolution window — the June 15 Oman round was canceled, not postponed, and no publicly confirmed held session between named principals occurred before EOD June 16. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span> RESOLVE-NO; → supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move.</p>

      <p>The first formally convened high-level US–Iran negotiating session opened at the Bürgenstock resort in Switzerland on June 21 and ran approximately 18 hours before concluding June 22 with a joint Qatar–Pakistan mediator statement announcing a "roadmap" toward a final deal. Vice President JD Vance led the American delegation alongside Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner; Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi and Parliament Speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf represented Iran. Vance stated four deliverables: IAEA inspector access to Iran "this week," a Hormuz deconfliction mechanism, a Lebanon ceasefire coordination framework, and a structured negotiation process confirmed by ≥5 TIER1 sources — Al Jazeera, NBC News, CNBC, NPR, CBS News. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span> RESOLVE-YES; → supports <span class="cite">N1</span> without move; → supports <span class="cite">N3</span> without move.</p>

      <p>Commercial shipping through the Strait of Hormuz remained volatile and well below pre-war norms throughout June 10–23. The highest single-day count reached approximately 35 transits on June 21 — up from near-zero at the start of the reporting window — before collapsing to approximately 12 on June 22 after Iran's military declared the strait closed (a statement its own foreign ministry contradicted and CENTCOM disputed). IMF PortWatch data through June 14 showed 0% of pre-war volume; even at the June 19 high of approximately 20 tankers, the 7-day moving average remained well below 15/day. A structural jump to the 50/day threshold before July 2 is blocked by active mines requiring 40–50 more days for clearance, P&amp;I war-risk surcharges not yet lifted, and Iran's contested re-closure on June 20. CENTCOM confirmed mine-clearance operations active since April 11. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span> p 0.20 → 0.10; → moved <span class="cite">F5</span> RESOLVE-YES; → supports <span class="cite">N2</span> without move.</p>

      <p>Within the June 10–24 window covered by <span class="cite">F3</span>, no confirmed bilateral Israel–Iran military exchange occurred. The June 7–8 missile exchange between Israel and Iran falls outside F3's observation window. Israel conducted strikes on Beirut's southern suburbs on June 14 — Lebanese territory, not Iranian — and Iran prepared a response that US intervention preempted; no Iranian missiles struck Israeli territory in this period and no IDF strikes hit Iranian territory after June 10. The ceasefire held through June 24 per Britannica's 2026 Iran war entry and NPR, in part through Trump's direct intervention with Netanyahu following the Beirut escalation. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span> RESOLVE-NO.</p>

      <p>On the Israeli domestic track, the Knesset dissolution bill's second and third readings remained pending as of June 23 — no plenum vote was held after the June 2 first reading's 106-0 passage. Netanyahu's coalition held at approximately 60 seats; polling aggregates showed the opposition approaching but not crossing the 61-seat threshold. Haredi parties (Shas, UTJ) continued publicly preferring a September election date while Netanyahu maintained his preference for October; all major parties are aligned on dissolution itself, making the date dispute a secondary intra-coalition negotiation rather than a blocking veto. A Times of Israel report from mid-June described Netanyahu as potentially pushing final readings "within days," leaving three weeks to the July 15 horizon. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span> p 0.45 → 0.55.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:20%;">Escalation 20%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:55%;">Protracted 55%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:25%;">De-esc 25%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (20%):</strong> Deal collapse — N1 fails (70%) compounded by working-group breakdown (N3 fails, 45%) and Hormuz re-closure holding; residual IRGC-hardline overreach risk drives new military exchange.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (55%):</strong> Base case — MOU and roadmap hold nominally; working groups convene but produce no final instrument within 60 days; Hormuz transit recovers partially but not to pre-war volume; Israeli domestic calendar grinds toward election.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (25%):</strong> N1 succeeds (30%) correlated with N2 succeeding (25%); IAEA inspectors operational; Hormuz 7-day average crosses 30/day; ceasefire holds without new Israeli strike cycle.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media (IRNA, Press TV, Tasnim) framed the June 17 MOU signing and the June 21–22 Switzerland session consistently as Iranian diplomatic achievements on favorable terms, emphasizing that negotiations occurred "without US preconditions" and that Iran's sovereignty remained intact. Supreme Leader Khamenei's June 18 written statement — which authorized the MOU — was published by IRNA with the caveat that engagement "does not mean accepting American views," a formulation designed to insulate the leadership from domestic hardline criticism. Fars News amplified this framing, presenting the 60-day ceasefire extension as Iran having obtained sanctions suspension without surrendering the nuclear file. → supports <span class="cite">N1</span> without move.</p>

      <p>On the IAEA question, Iranian state outlets carried Foreign Ministry spokesman Esmaeil Baghaei's June 22 rebuttal to Vance's statement, insisting cooperation with IAEA would continue "under the current procedures" — refusing to confirm Vance's characterization of new commitments. Press TV and Tasnim both ran this rebuttal prominently, framing the discrepancy as a US attempt to claim negotiating credit. This divergence in post-session public statements follows an established Iranian pattern of compartmentalizing the domestic audience — where IAEA access is politically sensitive among hardliners — from the external diplomatic track. → supports <span class="cite">N3</span> without move.</p>

      <p>On the Strait of Hormuz, Iranian state media produced contradictory outputs within 48 hours that document genuine institutional tensions rather than a coordinated messaging strategy: IRNA and Tasnim carried the IRGC Navy's June 20 declaration that the strait was closed, citing ceasefire violations; Press TV carried the Foreign Ministry's softer line stating shipping was operating normally. CENTCOM's public denial and the 55-ship transit count cited on June 21 were largely absent from Iranian state coverage. This internal divergence between military-hardline framing and diplomatic-track framing originates in genuine competition between IRGC and MFA institutional interests over control of the Hormuz narrative. → <span class="context-only">context only</span>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

